ful for and in  particular i think for the science  community and the space community to see  wally funk  uh you know almost 50 years after she  was in the mercury 13 program and passed  over because she was a woman  finally making it across their  boundaries so in that sense that's  historic  um but of course it's the sort of thing  we've been doing in space since the  1960s alan shepard  for who the new shepherd rocket has  launched uh named for he flew into space  in exactly the same way essentially in  1961  so in that sense i wouldn't call it  historic we're doing things we've done  before but  it's an interesting day uh in the space  community so  what do we need this so-called space  tourism for this this little hop which  is on your first test uh  so what what will it bring to the  scientific community what will do good  for the rest of us  well i think we need to be very clear  that these suborbital hops are very  different of course to what we do with  our astronauts what nasa does which  the russians do and the chinese which is  send astronauts into orbit that requires  a huge amount more energy  and is more dangerous because of that  and then they can go to the  international space station and do  science  on these very short flights a few  minutes of  microgravity in free fall there are some  science experiments you can do there but  it's really about the tourism  aspect but there's this phrase often  used about democratization of space and  that's something which i do chafe  against a bit because  our astronauts are democratically chosen  on the basis of their skills and their  talents not on the basis of how much  money they have and i think there's some  something very symbolic we're seeing two  of the richard's men in the world  having a bit of a contest to go and do  these flights and i think that's  something very different to what perhaps  many of us  hoped space would become over the last  50 years  well this this uh billionaire space race  does remind me a little about the  uh who could build the highest  skyscraper in new york and  at least that gave us i don't know the  empire state building and the chrysler  building what will be the legacy of this  thing  well that's hard to tell i mean many  people would say this is a moment where  if you don't build that technology you  can't then make it uh broader and apply  it to other people  but i think again we need to be very  clear this is the richest man in the  world who's made a lot of money during  the coronavirus crisis because we've all  been at home buying boxes full of his  stuff  which is great but you know is that  really the way we want  civil society to go again we've got an  astronaut in space at the moment  tomorrow pesky doing science  uh we've we're just in the process of  recruiting new astronauts the european  space agency so whether this will become  a diversion or really an opening of a  gateway  into something uh much bigger for the  whole of humankind i think really the  jury's out on that at this time  mark mccochran from the european space  agency thank you  very much for your insight
 

